Below is a guide showing  

1. Which tables / columns matter and why,  
2. Ten step‑wise BigQuery SQL statements (simple ➜ complex ➜ the final answer).  

=====================================================================
A.  RELEVANT TABLES & COLUMNS  
---------------------------------------------------------------------
bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.condition  
• plot_sequence_number : key to join with population / plot tables  
• condition_status_code : to report requested “condition status code”  
• subplot_proportion_unadjusted : basis for “subplot acres”  
• macroplot_proportion_unadjusted : basis for “macroplot acres”  

bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.population  
• plot_sequence_number : join key to condition  
• evaluation_group : evaluation‑group identifier we need to rank  
• evaluation_type : requested in the output  
• evaluation_description : requested in the output  
• state_code : state to be displayed  
• expansion_factor : converts proportions to acres  
• adjustment_factor_for_the_subplot : converts subplot proportion to acres  
• adjustment_factor_for_the_macroplot : converts macroplot proportion to acres  
• report_year_name : filter to year 2012 (contains list of report years)  

(Other columns such as stratum_code, etc., are not required for this task.)

=====================================================================
B.  EXAMPLE SQLs  
---------------------------------------------------------------------
Each query is wrapped with a short comment explaining the purpose.  
(Replace ⟨project⟩ with your GCP project if you copy‑paste.)  

```sql
-- 1) Peek at one evaluation group for 2012
SELECT DISTINCT evaluation_group, evaluation_description
FROM `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.population`
WHERE report_year_name LIKE '%2012%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Small sample of the condition table to inspect subplot / macroplot proportions
SELECT plot_sequence_number,
       condition_status_code,
       subplot_proportion_unadjusted,
       macroplot_proportion_unadjusted
FROM `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.condition`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Join one evaluation group and its conditions (tiny sample)
SELECT p.evaluation_group,
       c.condition_status_code,
       c.subplot_proportion_unadjusted
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.population` AS p
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.condition`  AS c
  ON   p.plot_sequence_number = c.plot_sequence_number
WHERE  p.evaluation_group = 42012 -- example id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Compute subplot acres for one record
SELECT c.plot_sequence_number,
       p.expansion_factor *
       COALESCE(p.adjustment_factor_for_the_subplot,1) *
       c.subplot_proportion_unadjusted           AS subplot_acres
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.population` AS p
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.condition`  AS c
  ON   p.plot_sequence_number = c.plot_sequence_number
WHERE  p.report_year_name LIKE '%2012%'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Same for macroplot acres
SELECT c.plot_sequence_number,
       p.expansion_factor *
       COALESCE(p.adjustment_factor_for_the_macroplot,1) *
       c.macroplot_proportion_unadjusted         AS macroplot_acres
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.population` AS p
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.condition`  AS c
  ON   p.plot_sequence_number = c.plot_sequence_number
WHERE  p.report_year_name LIKE '%2012%'
  AND  c.macroplot_proportion_unadjusted IS NOT NULL
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Compute both acres together for every condition in 2012
SELECT p.evaluation_group,
       c.condition_status_code,
       p.evaluation_type,
       p.state_code,
       p.expansion_factor *
       COALESCE(p.adjustment_factor_for_the_macroplot,1) *
       c.macroplot_proportion_unadjusted         AS macroplot_acres,
       p.expansion_factor *
       COALESCE(p.adjustment_factor_for_the_subplot,1) *
       c.subplot_proportion_unadjusted           AS subplot_acres
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.population` AS p
JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.condition`  AS c
  ON   p.plot_sequence_number = c.plot_sequence_number
WHERE  p.report_year_name LIKE '%2012%';
```

```sql
-- 7) Per evaluation group, keep only the single condition with the largest subplot acres
WITH cond_acres AS (
  SELECT p.evaluation_group,
         p.evaluation_type,
         p.evaluation_description,
         p.state_code,
         c.condition_status_code,
         p.expansion_factor *
         COALESCE(p.adjustment_factor_for_the_macroplot,1) *
         c.macroplot_proportion_unadjusted AS macroplot_acres,
         p.expansion_factor *
         COALESCE(p.adjustment_factor_for_the_subplot,1) *
         c.subplot_proportion_unadjusted   AS subplot_acres,
         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY p.evaluation_group
                            ORDER BY p.expansion_factor *
                                     COALESCE(p.adjustment_factor_for_the_subplot,1) *
                                     c.subplot_proportion_unadjusted DESC) AS rn
  FROM   `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.population` AS p
  JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.condition`  AS c
    ON   p.plot_sequence_number = c.plot_sequence_number
  WHERE  p.report_year_name LIKE '%2012%'
)
SELECT *
FROM   cond_acres
WHERE  rn = 1
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Count how many evaluation groups qualify
WITH cond_acres AS (
  SELECT p.evaluation_group,
         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY p.evaluation_group
                            ORDER BY p.expansion_factor *
                                     COALESCE(p.adjustment_factor_for_the_subplot,1) *
                                     c.subplot_proportion_unadjusted DESC) AS rn
  FROM   `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.population` AS p
  JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.condition`  AS c
    ON   p.plot_sequence_number = c.plot_sequence_number
  WHERE  p.report_year_name LIKE '%2012%'
)
SELECT COUNT(*) AS eval_groups_2012
FROM   cond_acres
WHERE  rn = 1;
```

```sql
-- 9) Rank evaluation groups by that maximum subplot acres
WITH cond_acres AS (
  SELECT p.evaluation_group,
         p.evaluation_type,
         p.evaluation_description,
         p.state_code,
         c.condition_status_code,
         p.expansion_factor *
         COALESCE(p.adjustment_factor_for_the_macroplot,1) *
         c.macroplot_proportion_unadjusted AS macroplot_acres,
         p.expansion_factor *
         COALESCE(p.adjustment_factor_for_the_subplot,1) *
         c.subplot_proportion_unadjusted   AS subplot_acres,
         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY p.evaluation_group
                            ORDER BY p.expansion_factor *
                                     COALESCE(p.adjustment_factor_for_the_subplot,1) *
                                     c.subplot_proportion_unadjusted DESC) AS rn
  FROM   `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.population` AS p
  JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.condition`  AS c
    ON   p.plot_sequence_number = c.plot_sequence_number
  WHERE  p.report_year_name LIKE '%2012%'
)
SELECT *
FROM   cond_acres
WHERE  rn = 1
ORDER BY subplot_acres DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) FINAL ANSWER: top‑10 evaluation groups in 2012 by the *largest* subplot acres
WITH cond_acres AS (
  SELECT p.evaluation_group,
         p.evaluation_type,
         c.condition_status_code,
         p.evaluation_description,
         p.state_code,
         p.expansion_factor *
         COALESCE(p.adjustment_factor_for_the_macroplot,1) *
         c.macroplot_proportion_unadjusted AS macroplot_acres,
         p.expansion_factor *
         COALESCE(p.adjustment_factor_for_the_subplot,1) *
         c.subplot_proportion_unadjusted   AS subplot_acres,
         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY p.evaluation_group
                            ORDER BY p.expansion_factor *
                                     COALESCE(p.adjustment_factor_for_the_subplot,1) *
                                     c.subplot_proportion_unadjusted DESC) AS rn
  FROM   `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.population` AS p
  JOIN   `bigquery-public-data.usfs_fia.condition`  AS c
    ON   p.plot_sequence_number = c.plot_sequence_number
  WHERE  p.report_year_name LIKE '%2012%'          -- restrict to 2012
)
SELECT evaluation_group,
       evaluation_type,
       condition_status_code,
       evaluation_description,
       state_code,
       macroplot_acres,
       subplot_acres
FROM   cond_acres
WHERE  rn = 1                        -- only the largest‑subplot condition per group
ORDER BY subplot_acres DESC
LIMIT 10;
```

The last query (Query 10) returns exactly what the question asks: the ten evaluation groups from 2012 with the greatest subplot acres, measured on the single condition (within each group) that has the largest subplot‑acre value, and displays all requested fields.
